CAT = "cmaProcedures"; SEG = "PRO"
Q = [
("The provider taps over the patient's abdomen with the fingers to hear whether it sounds hollow or dull. Which examination method is this?",
 "Percussion", ["Palpation", "Auscultation", "Inspection"],
 "Percussion is tapping to produce sounds that show whether underlying tissue is air-filled, fluid-filled or solid. Palpation is feeling with the hands."),
("Measuring a patient's height and the range of motion of a joint are examples of which examination method?",
 "Mensuration", ["Manipulation", "Percussion", "Auscultation"],
 "Mensuration means measuring, for example height, weight, head circumference or a joint's angle. Auscultation is listening, usually with a stethoscope."),
("The provider presses gently on the abdomen to feel for tenderness and enlarged organs. Which method is this?",
 "Palpation", ["Inspection", "Percussion", "Mensuration"],
 "Palpation uses the hands to feel texture, size, temperature and tenderness. Inspection uses the eyes only."),
("The patient kneels on the table with the chest down and hips raised for a sigmoidoscopy. Which position is this?",
 "Knee-chest", ["Lithotomy", "Prone", "Dorsal recumbent"],
 "Knee-chest gives access to the rectum and lower colon. It is hard to hold, so the patient is helped into it only when the provider is ready."),
("The provider needs to examine the back of a patient's spine. Which position places the patient face down?",
 "Prone", ["Supine", "Sims'", "Fowler's"],
 "Prone means lying flat on the abdomen. Supine is flat on the back."),
("How is a patient placed in the dorsal recumbent position?",
 "On the back, knees bent", ["On the left side, right knee bent", "Face down with arms at sides", "On the back, feet in stirrups"],
 "Dorsal recumbent is supine with knees flexed and feet flat on the table, which relaxes the abdomen. With the feet in stirrups it becomes lithotomy."),
("The provider orders skin staples removed from a healed incision. How is the staple remover used?",
 "Lower jaw under, then squeeze", ["Upper jaw under, then pull up", "Cut the staple in the middle", "Lift with forceps and twist"],
 "The lower jaw slides under the staple's center and squeezing the handles bends it so both prongs lift out of the skin. Staples are not pulled straight up."),
("A patient with a new arm cast asks what to watch for at home. Which sign should be reported right away?",
 "Numb or cold fingers", ["Mild itching under the cast", "A heavier feeling arm", "Dry skin at the cast edge"],
 "Numbness, tingling, coldness, pale or blue fingers or rising pain can mean the cast is cutting off circulation or pressing on a nerve. Mild itching is common."),
("When an arm sling is applied, where should the hand be?",
 "Slightly higher than the elbow", ["Hanging below the elbow", "Level with the shoulder", "Resting low on the opposite hip"],
 "Keeping the hand a little above the elbow reduces swelling and supports the wrist. A hand hanging below the elbow collects fluid."),
("How does surgical asepsis differ from medical asepsis?",
 "It keeps an area free of all microbes", ["It only reduces microbe numbers", "It is used for routine exams", "It needs no sterile supplies"],
 "Surgical (sterile) technique keeps an area free of all microorganisms, as for minor surgery. Medical asepsis, such as hand hygiene and cleaning, reduces their number and spread."),
("When putting on sterile gloves, how does the medical assistant pull on the second glove?",
 "Gloved fingers under its cuff", ["Bare hand inside its cuff", "Gloved thumb outside its cuff", "Grasp its palm with bare hand"],
 "The gloved hand touches only the sterile outside of the second glove by slipping its fingers under the folded cuff. Touching it with a bare hand contaminates it."),
("How is antiseptic applied to the skin before a minor surgical procedure?",
 "In circles, site outward", ["From the outside inward", "Back and forth over the site", "Dabbed on, then blotted dry"],
 "Working outward in widening circles moves organisms away from the incision site. Going back over cleaned skin with the same swab recontaminates it."),
("When opening a sterile pack on a Mayo stand, which flap is opened first?",
 "The flap farthest away", ["The flap closest to you", "The left side flap", "The right side flap"],
 "The far flap is opened first so the arm never reaches over an exposed sterile area. The flap nearest the body is opened last."),
("What does a healthy colostomy stoma look like?",
 "Moist and pink to red", ["Dry and dark purple", "Pale and gray", "Black at the edges"],
 "A healthy stoma is moist and pink to beefy red, like the inside of the mouth. Dark, pale or black tissue means poor blood supply and is reported."),
("A patient asks when to empty his ostomy pouch. What is the usual advice?",
 "At one-third to one-half full", ["Only when it is completely full", "Once a week on the same day", "Only after every meal"],
 "Emptying early keeps the weight of the pouch from pulling the seal loose and causing leaks. A full pouch can detach."),
("What is a hemostat used for during a minor procedure?",
 "Clamping blood vessels", ["Holding a suture needle", "Cutting suture material", "Retracting skin edges"],
 "A hemostat has locking jaws that clamp vessels to stop bleeding. A needle holder grips the curved suture needle."),
("Which instrument grips the curved needle while the provider places sutures?",
 "Needle holder", ["Tissue forceps", "Allis clamp", "Towel clamp"],
 "A needle holder has short, strong, locking jaws designed to hold a suture needle steady. Tissue forceps grasp tissue, not needles."),
("What does a biological indicator placed in an autoclave load contain?",
 "Live bacterial spores", ["Color-changing chemical ink", "A heat-sensitive wax pellet", "A sample of patient blood"],
 "The biological indicator holds heat-resistant bacterial spores. If the spores do not grow after the cycle, sterilization conditions were met. Tape only shows heat exposure."),
("Which information is written on the label of a wrapped instrument pack before autoclaving?",
 "Date, contents and initials", ["Patient name and diagnosis code", "Cost of the instruments", "The provider's signature"],
 "Labels show the sterilization date, what is inside and who wrapped it (often the load number). The date lets staff rotate stock and trace a failed cycle."),
("How are wrapped packs placed in the autoclave chamber?",
 "On edge with space between", ["Stacked flat and tight", "Pressed against the walls", "Wrapped together in plastic"],
 "Steam must circulate and reach every surface, so packs stand on edge with space between them. Tightly stacked packs trap air and may not sterilize."),
("Which body cavity holds the heart and lungs?",
 "Thoracic cavity", ["Abdominal cavity", "Pelvic cavity", "Cranial cavity"],
 "The thoracic cavity is above the diaphragm and holds the heart, lungs and great vessels. The abdominal cavity holds the stomach, liver and intestines."),
("A provider describes a sagittal image of the brain. How does the sagittal plane divide the body?",
 "Into right and left parts", ["Into front and back parts", "Into upper and lower parts", "Into inner and outer parts"],
 "The sagittal plane runs vertically from front to back, splitting right from left. The frontal (coronal) plane splits front from back."),
("At about what age do most children take their first independent steps?",
 "Around 12 months", ["Around 6 months", "Around 3 months", "Around 24 months"],
 "Most children walk alone between about 9 and 15 months, with 12 months typical. Sitting without support usually comes at about 6 months."),
("A patient has a red, hot, very painful big toe joint after a night of heavy eating and drinking. Which disorder causes this?",
 "Gout", ["Osteoarthritis", "Bursitis", "Carpal tunnel syndrome"],
 "Gout is caused by uric acid crystals in a joint and often strikes the big toe suddenly. Osteoarthritis develops slowly from cartilage wear."),
]
